  "title": "Beyond two values, blurring lets a law compose like cycles and count hard",
  "subject": "Whether a law on more than two values can compose its knots like cycles and still be hard to count, so as to select in PINNING C's sense",
  "conclusion": "The known cycle-composing laws are coset laws with uniform fibres, so they count easily. Doubling a value keeps a law's failures and circuits but makes its count #P-complete, so blurred parity (three values) and blurred Z3 (four values) compose like cycles, fail recognisably and count hard; every Mal'tsev law on three values composes like cycles.",
